.TH "DOMPOINTREADONLY.TOJSON" "3JS" "October 30, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
DOMPointReadOnly.toJSON \- DOMPointReadOnly: toJSON() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]toJSON()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]DOMPointReadOnly\f[R] interface returns a JSON\-serializable plain
object representing the \f[CR]DOMPointReadOnly\f[R] object.
.PP
The method is automatically called by \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R] when a
\f[CR]DOMPointReadOnly\f[R] object is encountered during JSON
serialization.
The serialized value can then be deserialized using the
\f[CR]DOMPointReadOnly.fromPoint()\f[R] function within the reviver of
\f[CR]JSON.parse()\f[R].
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
toJSON()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
A JSON\-serializable plain object containing the following properties:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]x\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]y\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]z\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]w\f[R]
.PP
Each property\(cqs value is copied as\-is.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Calling toJSON() directly
Calling \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] directly returns a plain object containing
the \f[CR]DOMPointReadOnly\f[R] object\(cqs properties.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] point = \f[B]new\f[R] DOMPointReadOnly(10, 20);

\f[B]const\f[R] json = point.toJSON();
console.log(json);
\f[I]// { x: 10, y: 20, z: 0, w: 1 }\f[R]
console.log(\f[B]typeof\f[R] json); \f[I]// \(dqobject\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.SS Serializing to a JSON string
The \f[CR]DOMPointReadOnly\f[R] object from the previous example is
serialized using \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R], which calls the
\f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] method automatically.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] pointJSON = JSON.stringify(point);
console.log(pointJSON);
.EE
.PP
This would log a JSON string like so (formatted for readability):
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqx\(dq: 10,
  \(dqy\(dq: 20,
  \(dqz\(dq: 0,
  \(dqw\(dq: 1
}
.EE
